    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28993",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={45}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot 315=14\\,175.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
