    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-76488",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={150}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${150}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={150}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/d61c6dbd94be14f2a455791a584a00f19ab7b52452b3acda984c5b278a5e39ed.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=150\\cdot 210=31\\,500.\n$$",
