    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-120476",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Equations With No Solutions",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations I, II, and III have no solutions.",
