  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3527",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x <             2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\cos{x} < 0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/0e370925faa2a4abc7ac613657ecdf27c2fa0eaa66cff05b077f77a71999c742.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4260",
      "knowledge_point_id": "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles",
      "problem": "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x <                2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=1.230\\,959…≈1.231\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 1.231\\,0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.231\\,0 \\\\  & =5.052\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3529",
      "knowledge_point_id": "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq  x <            180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 45^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3532",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Equations With No Solutions",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi?$\n\n- $\\sec x  = -1$\n\n- $\\sec x  = 2$\n\n- $\\sec x  = \\dfrac 1 3$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
